# Exercise: Channel Patterns
#
# Producer–consumer is the canonical channel pattern: one side
# sends values, the other receives them. The producer below
# sends three integers (1, 2, 3) and then drops its reference
# with `chan.close`. Your job is to receive those values and
# sum them.
#
# `chan.receive` has stack effect ( Channel -- T Bool ): it
# consumes the channel reference and returns the value plus a
# success flag. To receive *more than once* from the same
# channel you need to keep a reference around — `dup`, `over`,
# and `rot` are the usual moves.
#
# Heads up — there is no `while` combinator (see exercise 05).
# For an *unknown*-length stream you'd write a recursive helper
# that branches on the flag. Here we know exactly three values
# were sent, so three explicit receives is simpler and clearer.
#
# (Aside: the test holds an extra reference to the channel from
# `dup produce-values`, so even after the producer's `chan.close`
# the channel isn't *fully* closed — the runtime sees that the
# outer test strand still has a sender reference. That's why a
# fourth receive on this channel would block instead of returning
# false. Hardcoding the count avoids the issue.)
#
# Your task: write `sum-from-channel` — receive three values
# and return their sum.
#
# Stack walk:
#
#   ( chan )
#   dup chan.receive drop      # ( chan 1 )    receive #1, drop the flag
#   over chan.receive drop     # ( chan 1 2 )  copy chan back to top
#   rot chan.receive drop      # ( 1 2 3 )     chan goes to top, gets consumed
#   i.+ i.+                    # ( 6 )
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: produce-values ( Channel -- )
    dup 1 swap chan.send drop
    dup 2 swap chan.send drop
    dup 3 swap chan.send drop
    chan.close
;

: sum-from-channel ( Channel -- Int )
    # Your code here
    drop 0
;

: test-patterns ( -- )
    chan.make
    dup produce-values
    sum-from-channel

    # Do not edit below this line
    6 test.assert-eq
;
